    {
      "id": "1f86d710b7dc1fd9",
      "text": "&lt;p&gt;Self-described moderates say Ed Davey’s party offers them a home as Conservatives shift further right&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Climbing the party-branded stairs of the Brighton Centre with a Liberal Britain tote bag over his shoulder, Cody Butler looks quite at home at the Liberal Democrats’ annual jamboree.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;But asked how he is finding it, the 21-year-old replied: “Odd.” The former Tory activist resigned as chair of the One Nation Conservative Network and left his old party in July, arguing that centrists, remainers and members worried about the climate crisis and conservation were no longer welcome in the party. A week ago, &lt;a href=\"https://x.com/codybutleruk/status/2097745878991667249?s=20\"&gt;he joined the Lib Dems&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/sep/20/young-tory-defectors-lib-dems-conference\"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;",
